            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "include/sdbusplus/message/native_types.hpp",
                    "line": 98,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "We're accepting base by template, and not even rvalue?  What prevents constructing a bad dbus path this way if we allow potential user input in this constructor?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/sdbusplus/message/native_types.hpp",
                    "line": 99,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I like this concept overall, but could we make this API not require a base?\n\n\nFor example:\nsdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/openbmc_project\", \"inventory\");\n\nis harder to understand as an API than\n\nsdbusplus::message::object_path(\"xyz\", \"openbmc_project\", \"inventory\");\n\nThe prior API could potentially be used unsafely.\n\nsdbusplus::message::object_path(\"//bad_path\", \"inventory\");\n\nAnd still result in a crash, where the later API can't."
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/sdbusplus/message/native_types.hpp",
                    "line": 99,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "We should strive to avoid constructing invalid values, but also need to avoid creating unintended or confusing results.\n\nExample without a base:\n\n```\nconst auto obj2 = sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory/\", \"abc\");\n```\n\nresults in \n\n```\n/_2fxyz_2finventory_2f/abc\n```\n\nI worry that if we just append everything to `/` without checking, it will will be inconsistent with the existing constructor `sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory\")` which would then give a different result in\n\n```\nsdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory\", \"sensors\") \n\n!= sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory/sensors\")\n```\n\nWhat if we checked `base[0] == '/'` to detect an object path instead of path segment and handle it differently, would it work for you?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/sdbusplus/message/native_types.hpp",
                    "line": 99,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> We should strive to avoid constructing invalid values, but also need to avoid creating unintended or confusing results.\n> \n> Example without a base:\n> \n> ```\n> const auto obj2 = sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory/\", \"abc\");\n> ```\n> \n> results in \n> \n> ```\n> /_2fxyz_2finventory_2f/abc\n\n> ```\n\nYes, but that is a valid path, and any person looking at it will clearly know they messed up.  That API is also safe to use on fuzzers.\n\n> \n> I worry that if we just append everything to `/` without checking, it will will be inconsistent with the existing constructor `sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory\")` which would then give a different result in\n> \n> ```\n> sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory\", \"sensors\") \n> \n> != sdbusplus::message::object_path(\"/xyz/inventory/sensors\")\n\n> ```\n\nI'm confused, neither of the above is the API i suggested.  I suggested\n```\nobject_path(\"xyz\", \"inventory\", \"sensors\");\n```\n\nThe advantage there is that each element of that is directly able to be constructed from untrusted data (config file read, ect).\n\n> \n> What if we checked `base[0] == '/'` to detect an object path instead of path segment and handle it differently, would it work for you?\n\nthat would fail for an invalid path like \"//\"\n\n\nWhat I'd really like is a std::format equivalent, because that's relatively well defined behavior, we can check the static part of the strings at compile time, and it's an API that users are familiar with.  Boost::urls::format does something very similar for URIs that is effective.\n\nAnywho, this is merged already, and I don't care enough to revert."
                }
            ],
